WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
When an engineering team relies on manual steps

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.920
to release a mobile application,

3
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.120
they are building a brittle system.

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.440
With just one missed command or forgotten configuration,

5
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.760
the entire deployment process forcefully

6
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.680
and abruptly ceases up.

7
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.560
Compiling a production build directly

8
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.320
from a developer's laptop introduces invisible variables.

9
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.880
Local environment discrepancies guarantee configuration drift,

10
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.320
and untested dependencies can silently leak

11
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.000
excessive permissions into the final product.

12
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.280
React native compounds this complexity

13
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.880
because the build process must eventually fork.

14
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.160
A single JavaScript codebase must be packaged

15
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.240
into two distinctive artifacts,

16
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.800
an IPA for iOS and an AAB for Android.

17
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.880
To strip away the risk of human error,

18
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.840
a production-grade mobile release requires

19
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.000
a rigid, fully automated, and deterministic pipeline.

20
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.560
GitHub Actions provides the ideal infrastructure layer

21
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.520
to host this automation, executing predefined workflows

22
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.400
in isolated, reproducible server environments.

23
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.400
Inside those runner environments,

24
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.280
Fastlane serves as the orchestration layer.

25
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.840
It uses a composable Ruby DSL to abstract away

26
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.160
the dense, complex commands required by underlying native tools

27
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.640
like XcodeBuild and RattleU.

28
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.960
This outlines the architecture of a seven gate pipeline

29
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.120
from linting to distribution.

30
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.360
The sequence logic is absolute.

31
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.040
Every gate must pass successfully before the next executes.

32
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.640
The primary rule is strict isolation.

33
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.360
After code is pushed, zero human interaction is permitted.

34
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.360
By fully removing the human element from the deployment path

35
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.720
and replacing it entirely with rigid, automated gates,

36
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.280
ensures that every binary delivered to a user

37
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.320
is exactly the binary you intended to build.

38
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.760
The very first step in this automated sequence

39
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.720
is establishing identity.

40
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.560
The pipeline must parse the codebase

41
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.240
to determine exactly which version of the application

42
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.360
it is building.

43
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.120
This table breaks down the two distinct version numbers

44
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.520
in every native app.

45
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.960
The semantic version string is the human readable identifier

46
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.480
like 1.3.0 displayed to users.

47
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.280
The build code is a unique integer used internally

48
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.520
by application stores for binary tracking

49
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.360
and crash-symbolication.

50
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.360
While the semantic version stays the same

51
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.280
across multiple release candidates,

52
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.320
the integer build number operates under a strict rule.

53
00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.280
It must increment on every single upload attempt,

54
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.480
even if the previous upload failed.

55
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.800
To prevent discrepancies,

56
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.640
the pipeline designates the root package.json file

57
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.560
as the single source of truth.

58
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.600
Fastlane automatically parses this file

59
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.320
to extract both the semantic version

60
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.080
and the build number before compilation begins.

61
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.560
Once locked, the workflow reaches the build gate,

62
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.240
splitting into two concurrent tracks

63
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.880
for each operating system.

64
00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.720
Because Apple requires Xcode,

65
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.080
GitHub actions provisions a Mac OS runner for the iOS build.

66
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.320
Simultaneously, a new Ubuntu runner executes

67
00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.680
the Android build track.

68
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.760
Deriving the version metadata from a single file

69
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.960
across these two distinct parallel environments

70
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.320
ensures that when both binaries finally reach the user,

71
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.240
their version states are perfectly synchronized.

72
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.840
With the binaries compiled,

73
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.880
the pipeline enters the most notoriously fragile phase

74
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.640
of mobile CI workflows, cryptographic code signing.

75
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.200
Native operating systems enforce a strict security model.

76
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.520
They require every application to be sealed

77
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.560
with verified cryptographic credentials

78
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.280
before installation on a user's device.

79
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.760
Historically, engineering teams manually

80
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.320
past certificates.

81
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.920
That practice creates rapid exploration issues,

82
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.680
local keychain conflicts, and revoked profiles

83
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.080
that break builds.

84
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.640
Fastlane match exists to solve this exact bottleneck.

85
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.480
It is the modern standard for abstracting code signing away

86
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.880
from the vulnerable environment

87
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.200
of an individual developer's machine.

88
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.920
This diagram illustrates the match architecture.

89
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.080
Instead of decentralized files,

90
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.760
it creates a single central source of truth

91
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.960
for your entire organization.

92
00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.880
Match generates and stores all certificates

93
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.760
and provisioning profiles inside a dedicated,

94
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.760
heavily encrypted private Git repository.

95
00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.400
Because the CI runner operates as an isolated machine,

96
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.880
it needs a secure authentication mechanism

97
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.720
to access this repository autonomously during the pipeline run.

98
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.240
This is achieved by setting match Git basic authorization

99
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.120
as an environment variable in the CI workflow.

100
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.080
This base 64 encoded token grants read-only access.

101
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.720
It allows the runner to clone the private repo,

102
00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.160
decrypt the specific profiles it needs,

103
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.480
and sign the binary without requiring manual keychain manipulation.

104
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.120
By treating cryptographic certificates as sinkable Git state,

105
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.960
both the local developers and the remote CI runner

106
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.240
always pull from the exact same cryptographic source.

107
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.280
Centralizing credentials in this way

108
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.400
permanently eliminates the, it works on my machine,

109
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.840
signing error from production workflows.

110
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.080
Immediately following a successful signed compilation,

111
00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.440
the binary enters the pre-flight gate.

112
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.040
Standard unit tests and static code analysis

113
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.560
evaluate the source code.

114
00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:58.880
They are highly effective,

115
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.640
but they cannot inspect the final packaged native artifact.

116
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.680
This leaves the application vulnerable to permission creep,

117
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.120
the silent injection of dangerous native device requirements,

118
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.120
which often occurs when engineers update

119
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.440
third party react native libraries.

120
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.480
To catch these regressions before they reach

121
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.840
the app store reviewers,

122
00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.160
Fastlane executes a targeted inspection

123
00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.600
of the built artifact.

124
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.600
On the Android track,

125
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.520
the pipeline invokes a specific Android SDK tool

126
00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.680
called AAPT2.

127
00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.280
This utility forcibly unpacks the compiled Android asset bundle

128
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.640
to read its raw internal manifest.

129
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.720
This visual deaf shows the pre-flight core logic.

130
00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:38.200
Fastlane compares extracted permissions

131
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.240
against a known secure state,

132
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.680
defined by a repository text file called

133
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.640
the permissions baseline.

134
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.520
If Fastlane detects a mismatch,

135
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.360
like a new location access,

136
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.880
the pipeline registers a hard failure.

137
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.760
It halts the entire deployment process instantly,

138
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.800
blocking the potentially compromised binary

139
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.200
from moving to the distribution gate.

140
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.240
To proceed, human intervention is now required.

141
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.320
A developer must review the newly detected permission,

142
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.280
update the baseline text file to explicitly allow it,

143
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.200
and commit that change back to the repository.

144
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.760
Enforcing this rigid baseline

145
00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.720
acts as a final firewall.

146
00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.800
It guarantees that unvetted dependencies

147
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.280
cannot silently compromise user privacy

148
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.960
or trigger unexpected rejections

149
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.000
from App Store review teams.

150
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.240
With the binaries compiled, signed, and fully validated,

151
00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.400
the pipeline initiates the final stage,

152
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.040
pushing the artifacts to the distribution channels.

153
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.240
For iOS, Fastlane securely authenticates

154
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.440
with the App Store Connect API,

155
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.000
uploading the IPA directly to test flight

156
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.720
and managing phased release configurations.

157
00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.560
In parallel, the Android workflow

158
00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.080
leverages the Play Developer API to upload the AAB

159
00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.360
to the Google Play console, programmatically managing staged

160
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.360
rollouts and release tracks.

161
00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.560
If the team utilizes internal distribution

162
00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.040
for minor updates, the pipeline can optionally

163
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.920
route over the air bundles directly

164
00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.800
to self-hosted infrastructure.

165
00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.520
A single push of code traverses all seven gates successfully,

166
00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.840
routing assets to deployment targets.

167
00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.000
Post-release, the CI runner uploads

168
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.840
React Native Source Maps to Sentry

169
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.920
for Accurate Crash Symbolication.

170
00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.200
It then auto-generates a release changelog

171
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.720
from Git commits, dispatching a final Slack notification,

172
00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.640
architecting a deterministic seven gate pipeline

173
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.880
replaces fragile manual processes

174
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.160
with a zero-touch engineering system.

175
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.640
The result is a highly predictable deployment cycle

176
00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.200
that allows engineers to focus on code,

177
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.160
rather than the mechanics of releasing it.

